  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 100 AM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... The chance for storms returns to the forecast \nlate this week Friday and into early next week as a humid \nairmass returns.\n\nThe anomalous upper ridge over the region is forecast to \ngradually shift west toward the southern Plains of the US late \nthis week and weekend into the early part of next week. As that \nhappens, west to northwest flow in the mid-upper levels will \nallow for a frontal system to approach Friday into Saturday. \nMoisture levels will correspondingly increase to well above \nseasonal normals with precipitable water values around 2 inches \nFriday and Friday night. These moisture levels are expected to \nremain elevated near the 90th percentile for mid-September in \nthe 1.5 to 1.8 inch range through early next week.\n\nEnsemble and deterministic guidance has shown a more \nprogressive cold front on Friday compared to previous solutions,\nleading to an increase in storm chances late Friday into Friday\nnight. The front is expected to drop in from the northeast, \nthough perhaps stall out over the area Saturday before washing \nout sometime late this weekend Sunday. Shear profiles are quite \nunremarkable Friday, such that a severe threat is not expected \nat this point. But the boundary nearby will favor that isolated \nto scattered storm activity during peak heating. \n\nFrom Sunday onward, ensemble guidance continues to show a \npotential unsettled pattern, albeit with low predictability, for\nafternoon and evening isolated/scattered storms. The pattern is\nnot really conducive to widespread activity, but well above \naverage moisture levels will promote this storm chance. \n\nFor temperatures, we end the week generally in the lower 90s, \nfalling into mainly the mid 80s to around 90 through the \nremainder of the period. Dewpoints get soupy into the low 70s \nthis weekend, resulting in an end to the comfortable humidity.",
